 questions: [
  ["The characteristic shape of an early proximal carious lesion in enamel on a bitewing is:","A triangle with its base at the outer surface and apex toward the dentine",["A triangle with its base at the dentine and apex toward the outer surface","A thin band running along the enamel-dentine junction","A round radiolucency at the height of the contact point"],"Enamel prisms guide the spread of acid so the widest part lies at the outer surface. A band under the junction is the typical appearance of occlusal caries in dentine."],
  ["An occlusal carious lesion on 3.6 first appears on a bitewing as a thin radiolucent band below the enamel-dentine junction. The most accurate interpretation is that the lesion:","Has already reached dentine and is likely deeper than it appears",["Is confined to the outer half of the enamel","Has already reached the pulp and needs endodontics","Is an artefact of cusp overlap and can be ignored"],"Dense cuspal enamel hides early occlusal lesions, so a visible band means dentine is already involved. It does not by itself prove pulpal involvement, since depth needs assessment with other findings."],
  ["Which of the following best explains why radiographs underestimate the depth of a carious lesion?","Substantial mineral loss must occur before a change is visible on the image",["The X-ray beam magnifies the sound tissue around the lesion","Enamel fluoresces under X-rays and masks the lesion","The lesion scatters photons toward the receptor"],"Early demineralisation does not reduce absorption enough to show, so the histological front lies beyond the radiographic edge. Magnification is minimal with a bitewing and would not hide the lesion."],
  ["A 22-year-old has a clinically evident buccal lesion on 4.6, but the bitewing appears normal at that surface. The most likely reason is that:","The lesion is superimposed on sound tooth structure in the direction of the beam",["The bitewing was exposed with too short a cone length","The buccal enamel is thinner and transmits fewer photons","The lesion is too superficial to be seen in any intraoral view"],"The beam passes through buccal, dentine and lingual tissue, so sound tooth surrounding the lesion hides it. A clinical examination finds buccal and lingual surfaces better than a bitewing."],
  ["In health, the interproximal alveolar crest on a bitewing of a 25-year-old lies approximately:","1 to 2 mm apical to, and parallel with, a line joining adjacent CEJs",["5 to 6 mm apical to, and parallel with, a line joining adjacent CEJs","At the level of the contact point, and parallel to the occlusal plane","At the mid-root level, and perpendicular to a line joining adjacent CEJs"],"A crest 1 to 2 mm below and parallel to the CEJ line is normal, and more than about 2 mm suggests bone loss. Bone loss is judged against this reference, not against the occlusal plane."],
  ["A periapical shows the interproximal bone between 1.6 and 1.7 forming a defect whose base is apical to the crest of the adjacent bone. This is best described as:","Vertical (angular) bone loss",["Horizontal bone loss","Cervical burnout","A class I furcation lesion"],"A base apical to the neighbouring crest defines a vertical defect, which often reflects a local factor such as a plaque trap. Horizontal loss keeps the crest roughly parallel to the CEJ line."],
  ["A periapical of 3.6 shows a small dark wedge at the point where the roots divide, with widening of the PDL space at that point. The most likely finding is:","Furcation involvement",["Internal root resorption","Dens evaginatus","Cervical burnout"],"Bone loss in the inter-radicular area produces a dark wedge or widened PDL. Clinically this requires a furcation probe; a radiograph can underestimate the condition."],
  ["A 45-year-old has moderate generalised bone loss on radiographs with bleeding on probing. Which of the following can the radiographs not establish?","Whether the disease is currently active",["The pattern of interproximal bone loss","The presence of calculus at contact areas","The level of the crest compared with the CEJ"],"Radiographs record the cumulative result of past destruction, while activity is judged from probing and bleeding. Interproximal bone level and pattern are precisely what the image does show."],
  ["A 38-year-old with generalised skin tightening and a narrow mouth opening has uniform widening of the PDL space around many teeth. The most likely underlying condition is:","Systemic sclerosis",["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Paget disease of bone","Trauma from occlusion"],"Systemic sclerosis characteristically widens the PDL space uniformly around many teeth without much mobility. Occlusal trauma is more localised, and hyperparathyroidism more commonly causes generalised loss of lamina dura."],
  ["A 60-year-old with renal stones and raised serum calcium has generalised loss of lamina dura and a fine ground-glass trabecular pattern. The most likely diagnosis is:","Hyperparathyroidism",["Osteomalacia","Paget disease of bone","Fibrous dysplasia"],"Parathyroid hormone drives generalised bone resorption, and thin cortices with lost lamina dura are the radiographic result. Paget disease tends to give enlarged, cotton-wool bone with hypercementosis."],
  ["A 31-year-old has severe pain on biting on 2.6 that started 2 days ago, and cold testing gives no response. The periapical appears normal. Which of the following best explains this finding?","Too little mineral has yet been lost for a visible change",["The infection has drained through the sinus and cleared the bone","The apex lies outside the plane of the periapical beam","Acute inflammation deposits mineral that masks a lesion"],"Acute disease can precede detectable bone change by days or longer, so a normal image does not exclude apical periodontitis. Clinical tests carry the diagnosis at this stage."],
  ["A 19-year-old has a large carious lesion in 3.6 with a lingering response to cold. A diffuse radiopacity is seen at the mesial root apex with a widened PDL space. The most likely diagnosis is:","Condensing osteitis",["Idiopathic osteosclerosis","Hypercementosis","Periapical cemento-osseous dysplasia"],"Low-grade inflammation from an affected pulp stimulates bone formation at the apex. Idiopathic osteosclerosis occurs with a normal PDL and no pulpal cause, so a deep lesion and widened PDL space point away from it."],
  ["A 46-year-old woman has several radiolucent areas at the apices of 3.1, 3.2 and 4.1. The teeth are free of caries, and all respond normally to cold. The most appropriate management is:","Observation, as the findings suggest periapical cemento-osseous dysplasia",["Endodontic treatment of the three affected teeth","Apicectomy with biopsy of each of the three apices","Extraction of the three teeth with curettage"],"Multiple lucencies at vital lower incisors in a middle-aged woman fit this benign condition, which needs no endodontic treatment. Treating a vital tooth for an imagined pulpal lesion is the classic error."],
  ["A 3 mm periapical radiolucency on non-vital 1.2 and a 12 mm well-corticated lesion on non-vital 2.2 are compared. Which statement is most accurate about distinguishing granuloma from cyst?","Histology is needed, since imaging cannot reliably make the distinction",["Size above 5 mm reliably identifies a cyst on a radiograph","A corticated border reliably identifies a cyst on a radiograph","Loss of lamina dura reliably identifies a granuloma on a radiograph"],"Large corticated lesions are more likely to be cysts, but overlap means only histology confirms the type. Treatment of the necrotic pulp comes first in either case."],
  ["A periapical of 1.1 shows a lesion that stays centred on the canal when the tube is shifted, with a smoothly ballooned canal outline. The most likely diagnosis is:","Internal root resorption",["External cervical resorption","Vertical root fracture","Periapical granuloma"],"Internal resorption enlarges the canal itself, so it stays with the canal on a shift and the outline is lost into it. External lesions move away on a shift and the canal outline remains visible through them."]
 ]
